let's talk about Amazon you might have  seen the headlines over the weekend  Amazon paid no taxes federally on eleven  point two billion dollars in profits in  2018 in fact Amazon actually had a  negative federal tax rate last year  Amazon receiving a refund of a hundred  and twenty nine million dollars last  year which works out to a negative tax  rate now consider that the poorest 20  percent of American households paid 1.5  percent federal tax on average a couple  of years ago okay  Amazon with eleven billion dollars in  profits paid a negative one percent tax  rate how does Amazon respond to requests  for comment about this they say what  every single one of these companies says  which is we paid all of the taxes that  we are legally required to pay which of  course isn't the issue the issue is why  is it that the law allows for this to  take place and the answer is the  corporate lobbying industrial complex  the way that this happens because you  might say wait David the the corporate  tax rate was thirty five percent and  then Donald Trump dropped it to twenty  one percent how is it that Amazon paid  minus one percent taxes it doesn't make  sense well there's a couple different  reasons number one there is a massive  tax break that is given out for  executive stock options that saved  Amazon about half of all the taxes that  they would normally pay and then there's  a whole bunch of unspecified tax credits  of all kinds which are just like classic  lobbyist crony handouts and all of these  mechanisms were lobbied for by corporate  lobbyists we are a corporate sock recei  as I have been telling you so let's talk  about reactions to this first you have  some people on the right who are sort of  claiming to be angry about this even  though they seem to believe that  corporations having tax cuts is good for  the economy one example is that guy  charlie  jerk from Turning Point USA he's been  using the Amazon story just to get  clicks check out his double standard  like within hours he tweeted Alexandria  Cassio Cortez has done more damage to  her community than any representative in  recent memory  she killed a multi-billion dollar  investment meaning Amazon from coming to  New York and with it 25,000 middle-class  jobs she is a perfect socialist saying  Amazon is good and AOC shouldn't have  lobbied against it coming to Newt to New  York City and then just hours later  charlie Kirk criticizing Amazon for  paying zero in federal taxes despite 11  billion in earnings and saying if this  is cronyism so is Amazon good or bad  charlie because that entire New York  City headquarters was going to be a huge  tax hand out to them but he's saying AOC  did the wrong thing by preventing them  from coming to New York City totally  incoherent okay these people on the  right that are playing games with this  simply shouldn't be part of the  discussion the real question here is one  of supply side versus demand side okay  if you are given huge corporate tax  breaks for executive stock options that  encourages very specific behaviors  number one it encourages the company  Amazon to give more money to executives  rather than to give money to regular  workers this increases inequality this  shifts the tax burden away from  corporations and from executives and it  shifts it towards in proportion regular  workers  that's called supply-side economics or  trickle-down economics the idea that if  we have the corporation pay less tax and  we give the richest people at our  company more money that that is going to  magically trickle down and be good for  everybody a rising tide lifts all boats  sort of thing it's it's wrong I mean  it's just been widely debunked there is  no serious economic evidence supporting  that the counter-argument is hey we  should get rid of these tax breaks and  these deductions and these credits that  would then shift the burden to those who  can most afford it  and least need the additional money  which they would disproportionately  stick in a bank account anyway not  stimulating the economy the corporation  for example that profited to the tune of  11 billion dollars and the executives  should be bearing more of the tax burden  the individual workers often working at  Amazon under not great conditions they  should get more money which they would  likely spend in the economy and that  would be much better for the economic  ecosystem at large now this is not  likely to change soon even a lot of  Democrats don't have as a top priority  changing these kinds of deductions  exemptions and loopholes one idea that's  a very very interesting idea is a  minimum tax for corporations with  individuals there is something called  the alternative minimum tax which means  if you made a million dollars for  example and you can come up with  $900,000 in deductions which will get  you down to only paying taxes on  $100,000 you're not allowed to do that  because of something called the  alternative minimum tax which says it  doesn't matter how many deductions you  could take if this is your income there  is some minimum amount of tax that you  have to pay people like Donald Trump for  example a lot of the taxes that he does  pay are because of the alternative  minimum tax so the idea is an  alternative minimum tax for corporations  if a corporation has the revenues and  profits that Amazon has even if they  could put together cobble together  enough deductions and tax breaks and  credits to end up paying zero an  alternative or a corporate minimum tax  would force them to pay some minimum  level of tax which would make a really  big difference to the the programs that  could be funded out of those taxes it's  an idea  good luck getting Democrats and  Republicans to act together to actually  make that happen there are other ways  but I'm not going to go into them now it  should probably be a segment on its own  send me your thoughts  where  not only on so there's kind of two  issues here issue number 